prompt:
`if` runs one block of code or another, depending on a single TRUE or
FALSE. What does this print?

```r
map <- 106
if (map < 65) {
  print("low")
} else {
  print("adequate")
}
```

When a block is a single line, the curly brackets are sometimes left out:
`if (map < 65) print("low")` means the same as the version with brackets.
You'll see both in generated code.

One reading is missing. What happens?

```r
sbp <- c(118, NA, 131, 156)
for (reading in sbp) {
  if (reading > 140) print("high")
}
```
